WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.260
Do you ever get the feeling like you’re being watched?

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:07.220
The other day, I was thinking about buying
this blanket online. I put it in my cart,

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.700
but then kinda waffled 'cause it was expensive,
and then I got distracted and did something

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.430
else. I just received a personal
Facebook message from the company reminding

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.040
me that it's in my cart!
How did they do that? I don’t even use the

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.800
same email addresses for these accounts! Is Facebook listening

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:25.860
to me?

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.380
Don’t go getting fit for your tinfoil hat
just yet. There isn’t any hard evidence

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.780
that Facebook is surreptitiously accessing
your phone’s mic, and even if they could,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:39.920
the volumes of data they’d have to analyze
to eavesdrop on just their American users

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:47.499
would be about 20 petabytes per day… 33
times more than their total daily data consumption.

00:00:47.499 --> 00:00:53.170
Besides, online tracking technology is so
advanced these days, they don’t really need

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:56.740
to. You’re dropping bread crumbs everywhere
you go.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.759
Imagine if you went to a shopping mall and
had an employee follow you around upselling

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.920
the entire time:

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.880
Don’t you just love that shirt? Give me
your email and I’ll give you $5 off right

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:21.119
now! Hey, remember that yoga mat you looked
at last week? Well guess what, 5 other people

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:26.250
just bought it and say it’s the best ever!
10% off if you buy it right now! Hey, don’t forget

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.560
it’s your mom’s birthday next week. She’d
love this hilarious coffee mug! Or maybe

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.280
this fancy candle? Three for the price of
one if you just use this code!

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.660
No one likes being badgered by an overly excited
employee. But that’s basically what’s

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:43.300
happening when you shop online. And it can
take a serious toll on our bank accounts.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.580
So let’s get clear on what companies are
really doing and what we can do about it.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.619
The main tactic that e-tailers use is called
Online Behavioral Advertising. It’s pretty

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:58.781
darn sophisticated and getting more so every
day. Here’s how it works: First, a website

00:01:58.781 --> 00:02:03.850
will implant a small piece of data on your
computer known as a cookie. Sounds cute, but

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:08.470
this cookie comes with strings attached. It
tracks how you maneuver around a website.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.370
What pages you click on, how long you hang
out there, what you search for, what you add

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:13.460
to your cart.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:18.320
It then harvests this data and uses it to
build a profile. It tries to guess things

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:23.370
like your age, your income bracket, your likes
and dislikes all in hopes of serving you ads

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.900
that are the most likely to capture your attention.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:31.480
As new parents, we do most of our shopping
online, which makes us prime targets. Julia

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.190
was recently served up an ad for baby-booties
that don’t slip off.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:39.760
If you didn’t know, keeping socks or shoes
on babies’ feet is basically impossible,

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.100
so of course my attention was captured. I
clicked the link and wouldn’t ya know it...5

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:49.000
minutes later I was checking out. And now,
those dang shoes pop up on EVERY SITE I’M

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.640
ON! I feel like I can’t escape! Thanks,
cookies.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.140
This is a textbook example of “re-targeting,”
Only 2% of users will become a customer on

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:03.620
their first visit, so a cookie’s job is
to quietly follow you around cyberspace, waiting

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.180
for the right time to invite you back to their
store.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:11.160
If a retailer has gotten access to your contact
information like your email, you will also be

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.920
barraged with personalized ads and offers.
Amazon is the absolute king of this tactic.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.780
Say you buy a yoga mat, they will assume you
probably need this awesome yoga shirt, oh

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:27.450
and you gotta have yoga blocks and what about
yoga socks?! These approaches are super effective.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:33.180
A re-targeted ad is 70% more likely to become
a paying customer than a randomly served one.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.950
Amazon is also famous for leveraging something
called “social proof,” our tendency to

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:43.450
copy the actions of others because we assume
they know more than we do. This is why 87%

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.510
of users report that they use customer reviews
to decide on a purchase. Of course, because

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:53.570
this is the internet, this has led to an entire
hidden economy of sellers paying people to

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:57.980
write fake reviews for their products. Amazon
claims that the percentage of reviews that

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:02.070
are fraudulent is “tiny,” but they don’t
really have much of an incentive to crack

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.680
down on them because the more good reviews,
the more sales. There are websites that will

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:11.150
analyze a product page and tell you how many
of the reviews are suspect, but the methods

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.910
these sites use are not proven to be 100%
accurate.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:19.440
All this wouldn’t be so bad if going online
was something you did every once in a while.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.120
But according to a recent Nielsen study, Americans
spend approximately 11 hours PER DAY staring

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.130
at screens. That’s like spending half your
life in a shopping mall. It’s no surprise

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:35.140
that one out of every three dollars of discretionary
spending is being spent online.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:39.560
While avoiding online shopping altogether
probably isn’t realistic, there are things

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:45.100
we can do to minimize the creepy stalking
from retailers so we can shop more deliberately.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:49.770
First, set up an email account for
your online shopping. That way, all the spam

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.380
and promotional offers won’t be clogging
your primary inbox.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:58.140
Next, you can regularly delete cookies and
“un-remember” your credit card numbers

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:03.580
from your browser’s memory. I know, I know,
this will make online shopping less convenient

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.960
because you’ll have to keep re-entering
your info, but remember, we’re trying to

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:14.050
fight brainlessness and make purchasing as
intentional as possible. E-tailers want shopping

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.450
to be effortless, because they know that the
more steps involved, the more chances you’ll

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.360
have to realize that you don’t really need
those fancy yoga socks. If you’re not sure

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:26.760
how to do this, just google, "How to erase cookies in..." The name of your browser

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:31.880
And lastly, we strongly recommend setting
a hard cap for online expenditures by opening

00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:36.830
a separate checking account or online wallet
that’s only for shopping online. Top it

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.490
off with how much you want to allow yourself
to spend per month, and when it’s empty,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:45.070
well, you’ll just have to wait till next
month to get that that 4K Blu-Ray of The Matrix.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:50.030
I admit it, I love seeing those packages show
up at my door as much as the next person,

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:55.389
but for most people, shopping online is a
luxury. The vast majority of necessities like

00:05:55.389 --> 00:05:59.360
groceries and household supplies are still
purchased at brick and mortar stores.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.020
E-tailers want us to treat online shopping
like a normal, habitual part of lives, but

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:10.220
any discretionary spending should still be a special,
deliberate decision. You probably can’t

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:14.760
ever get away from online advertising, but
you can at least be aware of how it’s trying

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.880
to influence your behavior.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:18.420
And that’s our two cents!

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:23.840
Are there any other ways online advertisers are driving you crazy?

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.000
I'd love to hear about it in the comments.

